what REDS %734's free half. Its cause -- two concurrent rye builds sharing ephemeral .zig shadow names -- is captured and its repair is a compiler-bridge decision wanting Keaton's word. The row names a SECOND fault nobody owns: three of its thirteen captured failures said only 'Lattice build failed', because the assert discards ${build.err}, which is why a fortnight of these left no evidence to read. Every Rishi .ok comes from run -> { out, err, code, ok }, so the evidence always exists and the cure is one interpolation the tree already writes 1,708 times. Measured over 2,571 tracked .rish sources: 10,237 asserts on a run's .ok, of which 1,708 name their own .err or .err_brief, 462 name their own .out or .code, 550 point at a file on disk, and 7,517 are MUTE -- a fixed sentence with the run's own words discarded. On build asserts alone the split is 744 naming the error against 720 mute, so half the tree applies a cure the other half omits. Build the static census reading tracked bytes alone, classify the four answers apart, report mute_asserts as a ratchet under a ceiling that only falls so each lane sweeps its own files, prove it from both sides in a pen, and sweep only the cohort %734 itself names. No sweep of a peer's files, no shared file touched.
